00:58:37,470 --> 00:58:40,995
coming out of your
your father that never cried.

1021
00:58:41,822 --> 00:58:45,696
So, it wasn't easy,
but, uh, I was ready.

1022
00:58:49,351 --> 00:58:52,485
I came here June 11, 1991.

1023
00:58:53,617 --> 00:58:56,620
I had no prospects
of a job

1024
00:58:57,403 --> 00:58:59,274
and I was the sole provider.

1025
00:59:00,928 --> 00:59:02,539
It was very difficult.

1026
00:59:03,714 --> 00:59:06,586
I was able
to ask for a hamburger.

1027
00:59:07,108 --> 00:59:09,023
I had that much English.

1028
00:59:09,937 --> 00:59:12,418
And I had to work
in construction.

1029
00:59:12,461 --> 00:59:13,767
Actually,
it was not construction,

1030
00:59:13,811 --> 00:59:14,899
it was destruction.

1031
00:59:19,207 --> 00:59:20,644
I didn't have
the proper gear.

1032
00:59:21,645 --> 00:59:23,255
I was eating dust.

1033
00:59:24,430 --> 00:59:26,867
And one day,
one of those heavy doors

1034
00:59:26,911 --> 00:59:28,608
landed on my foot.

1035
00:59:28,652 --> 00:59:31,568
I could barely walk.

1036
00:59:32,830 --> 00:59:35,267
But, regardless
of the challenges,

1037
00:59:35,310 --> 00:59:37,051
I was happy.

1038
00:59:37,095 --> 00:59:39,445
I knew that I was on my way
to something better.

1039
00:59:40,794 --> 00:59:43,057
I got into
the University of Texas

1040
00:59:43,101 --> 00:59:45,277
for aerospace engineering.

1041
00:59:46,887 --> 00:59:50,195
Later, I came to JPL
asking for advice.

1042
00:59:51,544 --> 00:59:54,155
I came asking for advice,

1043
00:59:54,199 --> 00:59:57,506
and at the end he said
it was a really nice interview.

1044
00:59:59,813 --> 01:00:00,901
I couldn't believe it.

1045
01:00:02,076 --> 01:00:03,251
A couple of years later,

1046
01:00:04,209 --> 01:00:05,427
after graduation,

1047
01:00:06,472 --> 01:00:09,606
in May 16, 1998,

1048
01:00:10,868 --> 01:00:13,087
that's when I began
to work at JPL.

1049
01:00:28,494 --> 01:00:29,582
Voyager ace, 4-3.

1050
01:00:30,757 --> 01:00:32,716
4-3, Voyager ace.

1051
01:00:32,759 --> 01:00:35,240
Uh, yeah, we just
had a telemetry countage.

1052
01:00:35,283 --> 01:00:36,589
Uh, would you like
the?

1053
01:00:37,198 --> 01:00:39,374
Uh, copy that,
no, I got it as well.

1054
01:00:39,418 --> 01:00:40,506
Thank you.

1055
01:00:57,566 --> 01:01:00,265
And, uh, probably
we need to turn cameras off.

1056
01:01:00,308 --> 01:01:02,136
I need to go
and consult with

1057
01:01:26,421 --> 01:01:28,119
So, that's it.

1058
01:01:28,162 --> 01:01:29,903
So, I might want to check
on my phone throughout.

1059
01:01:29,947 --> 01:01:31,557
You're gonna have me sit down.
Is that the idea?

1060
01:01:31,600 --> 01:01:33,385
- Oh, yeah.
- Oh, yeah, okay.

1061
01:01:33,428 --> 01:01:35,213
So, I can hide I can hide
my phone in my pocket.

1062
01:01:35,256 --> 01:01:36,693
- Yes.
- Okay.

1063
01:01:44,744 --> 01:01:45,789
No.

1064
01:01:52,491 --> 01:01:53,666
You know, basically...

1065
01:01:54,711 --> 01:01:56,800
it didn't go
quite as we wanted it to.

1066
01:01:56,843 --> 01:01:59,716
So, we're working through
what it what did happen

1067
01:01:59,759 --> 01:02:02,153
and that's about as much as
I can tell you right now.

1068
01:02:06,418 --> 01:02:07,506
Correct.

1069
01:02:10,161 --> 01:02:11,815
That's, uh, to be determined.

1070
01:02:14,034 --> 01:02:15,949
We have to make sure
that Voyager 2 is okay

1071
01:02:15,993 --> 01:02:17,298
before we take
the antenna down.

1072
01:02:26,003 --> 01:02:27,787
Hello?

1073
01:02:27,831 --> 01:02:29,746
It's been really wild.

1074
01:02:29,789 --> 01:02:36,317
Um, basically, the mag-roll
did not ex well...

1075
01:02:37,623 --> 01:02:39,364
Certain things happened,

1076
01:02:39,407 --> 01:02:41,409
unusual things happened
during the mag-roll

1077
01:02:41,453 --> 01:02:42,976
that should have not happened.

1078
01:02:43,020 --> 01:02:45,326
Still,
I mean, we do not know

1079
01:02:45,370 --> 01:02:49,635
we don't know everything,
so we're working overtime.

1080
01:02:49,678 --> 01:02:51,637
Everybody's working overtime.

1081
01:03:02,866 --> 01:03:04,737
If you're driving a car
down the street

1082
01:03:04,781 --> 01:03:07,522
and the light changes red,
you step on the brake,

1083
01:03:07,566 --> 01:03:09,176
and in three-tenths
of a second,

1084
01:03:09,220 --> 01:03:10,961
the pedal is depressed
and you're stopping.

1085
01:03:11,962 --> 01:03:14,573
That's because you saw the light
change red right away.

1086
01:03:17,881 --> 01:03:20,840
If you're on a spacecraft
and something goes wrong,

1087
01:03:21,362 --> 01:03:23,103
we don't know about that

1088
01:03:23,147 --> 01:03:25,105
until the radio signal
that detected the problem

1089
01:03:25,149 --> 01:03:26,977
gets transmitted
back to the Earth.

1090
01:03:27,020 --> 01:03:28,805
Which can take hours.

1091
01:03:32,896 --> 01:03:35,159
And then you figure out
what happened on the Earth

1092
01:03:35,202 --> 01:03:36,421
and do something.

1093
01:03:45,865 --> 01:03:48,694
But engineers
are problem solvers,

1094
01:03:48,737 --> 01:03:50,783
that's what they wanna do,
they want to solve problems.

1095
01:03:50,827 --> 01:03:53,655
The tougher the problem,
the more they like it.

1096
01:04:02,447 --> 01:04:04,405
We're trying to recover

1097
01:04:04,449 --> 01:04:06,059
one step at a time.

1098
01:04:06,103 --> 01:04:08,801
This has been the craziest.

1099
01:04:08,845 --> 01:04:10,847
I mean, we've had scares,

1100
01:04:10,890 --> 01:04:14,154
we have things
that we have to react,

1101
01:04:14,198 --> 01:04:15,895
but very quickly, I mean,

1102
01:04:15,939 --> 01:04:18,158
nothing that has lasted
this long.

1103
01:04:18,202 --> 01:04:19,986
And we we don't know
what's going to happen.

1104
01:04:59,808 --> 01:05:01,071
With the heaters off,

1105
01:05:01,114 --> 01:05:03,290
it's a race against time.

1106
01:05:03,943 --> 01:05:05,379
If the hydrogen freezes,

1107
01:05:05,423 --> 01:05:07,468
then the spacecraft
starts stumbling

1108
01:05:08,252 --> 01:05:10,428
and, uh, we lose
the spacecraft forever.

1109
01:05:11,646 --> 01:05:13,910
It's a challenge
on top of a challenge

1110
01:05:13,953 --> 01:05:15,433
because with Voyager 2,

1111
01:05:16,347 --> 01:05:18,915
the command frequency
changes with temperature.

1112
01:05:19,916 --> 01:05:22,831
Since the spacecraft temperature
is going down,

1113
01:05:22,875 --> 01:05:25,356
we had to figure out
the right frequency.

1114
01:05:26,705 --> 01:05:30,448
But you don't know how fast
the temperature is changing.

1115
01:05:30,970 --> 01:05:33,364
We have to make
the best guess we can.

1116
01:05:35,757 --> 01:05:36,845
It's very stressful.

1117
01:05:37,324 --> 01:05:38,499
The time is critical.

1118
01:05:41,720 --> 01:05:43,983
Finally,
we sent a command.

1119
01:05:48,118 --> 01:05:51,773
Turn gyros off
and heaters back on.

1120
01:05:53,688 --> 01:05:57,518
We were crossing our fingers,
uh, hoping for the best.

1121
01:05:58,955 --> 01:06:00,695
Everybody's
looking at the monitors

1122
01:06:00,739 --> 01:06:02,436
waiting for the signal.

1123
01:06:03,481 --> 01:06:05,004
It took 35 hours.

1124
01:06:06,049 --> 01:06:08,921
But finally it said
that the commands were received

1125
01:06:08,965 --> 01:06:10,009
by the spacecraft.

1126
01:06:17,669 --> 01:06:20,150
It was just amazing.
I mean, it's just beautiful.

1127
01:06:22,108 --> 01:06:24,893
Phew,
the relief that you feel

1128
01:06:24,937 --> 01:06:26,504
to see that,

1129
01:06:26,547 --> 01:06:31,161
"Okay, we were able to save
the spacecraft.

1130
01:06:41,562 --> 01:06:46,132
We were just about
to put the spacecraft

1131
01:06:46,176 --> 01:06:49,266
back into its normal
configuration

1132
01:06:49,309 --> 01:06:50,745
in terms of temperature.

1133
01:06:51,833 --> 01:06:53,400
All of a sudden,
they just said,

1134
01:06:53,444 --> 01:06:58,840
"Okay, grab everything
and go home."

1135
01:06:58,884 --> 01:07:00,494
New alarm bells
ringing tonight

